# Protocol working state — installed by .rokct/initiate.py, not committed.
.rokct/skills/
.rokct/tmp/

# The vendored SDK cache is COMMITTED — with `git add -f`, because it is
# deliberately listed here. Vercel composes from it at build time
# (vercel.json buildCommand -> scripts/compose.sh) with no token, no clone
# and no network, so it has to be in the commit. The ignore rule exists for
# one reason: the fleet linter's auto-fix runs
# `prettier --write . --ignore-path .gitignore` and commits the result, and
# reformatting a vendored template would change the cache content
# .rokct/lock.json pins — the next build would refuse to compose. That pass
# honours nothing but this file, so the shield lives here and
# `scripts/compose.sh refresh` stages the cache with -f. Tracked files stay
# tracked regardless of an ignore rule; the lock's content_sha256 fails the
# build loudly if a cache entry is ever incomplete or edited.
.rokct/cache/
# The install record `scripts/compose.sh` writes on every compose: this
# machine's own file digests, regenerated each run, never vendored. It sits
# inside .rokct/cache/ which the refresh stages with `git add -f`, so it is
# named here to keep that force-add from sweeping it into a commit - a
# committed copy drifts on the very next compose and fails the zero-drift check.
.rokct/cache/install_state.json
.rokct/sdk_installer_base.py

# Composed output — everything the SDK installers write into the host, listed
# in the generated block at the end of this file. The shell commits none of
# it: it is regenerated on every compose from the cache above.

